/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - At 1550 nm the material term Dm is about +21 ps/(nm·km) and fixed by the glass.
   - DSF has a weakness with wavelength-division multiplexing (WDM).
   - Non-zero dispersion-shifted fiber (NZDSF) keeps a small dispersion of about 2 to 6 ps/(nm·km) across the 1530 to 1565 nm band, positive or negative depending on the type.
   - The price is a smaller effective area Aeff, about 55 to 72 µm² against 80 µm² for standard fiber, so the light is more intense and other nonlinear effects are a little stronger.
   - Dispersion-compensating fiber (DCF) leaves the line fiber alone and adds a spool with strong negative dispersion, made with a small core and high Δ: D of about −80 to −100 ps/(nm·km) in common modules.
   - D₁, L₁ are the dispersion (ps/(nm·km)) and length (km) of the line fiber; D₂, L₂ those of the DCF, with D₂ negative.
   - For 100 km of standard fiber at +17 ps/(nm·km), 1700 ps/nm must be cancelled: 17 to 20 km of DCF at −100 to −85.
*/
