/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Rayleigh scattering is linear and elastic: the scattered light has the signal’s frequency and its share does not depend on power.
   - SBS.
   - SRS.
   - Only the first part of a span is bright enough to drive these effects, because the signal fades along the fiber.
   - Leff is the effective length in m; α is the attenuation coefficient in nepers per metre (dB/km ÷ 4343); L is the physical length in m.
   - The threshold is the launch power at which the Stokes wave grows as large as the signal at the output.
   - Pth,B ≈ 21 Aeff/(gBLeff) Pth,B is the SBS threshold in W; Aeff is the effective core area in m²; gB ≈ 5 × 10⁻¹¹ m/W is the peak Brillouin gain coefficient.
   - Pth,R ≈ 16 Aeff/(gRLeff) Pth,R is the SRS threshold in W; gR ≈ 7 × 10⁻¹⁴ m/W is the peak Raman gain coefficient near 1550 nm.
   - Many textbooks also give empirical forms in practical units, with d the core diameter in µm, λ in µm, αdB in dB/km and ν the source bandwidth in GHz: PB = 4.4 × 10⁻³ d²λ²αdBν watts and PR = 5.9 × 10⁻² d²λαdB watts.
*/
